# Build context for `docker build -f Dockerfile.gateway .` and `-f Dockerfile.engine .`.
#
# Both images install dependencies from pyproject.toml + uv.lock and copy only `src/`
# and `configs/` (plus README.md and LICENSE, which the package build reads), so
# everything below is either host-specific, regenerable, or pure weight: .venv/ alone is
# gigabytes of wheels built for this machine's CPython and CUDA, and .git/ carries the
# whole history to the daemon on every build. Keeping them out also means a future
# `COPY . .` cannot bake a host-specific virtualenv into an image.

# Version control and CI
.git/
.gitattributes
.gitignore
.github/

# Environments -- the images build their own under /opt/turboserve
.venv/
venv/
.env
.env.*

# Byte-code, build and tool caches
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
.eggs/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
coverage.xml
htmlcov/
.hypothesis/

# Not used by any image: measured results, prose, deployment manifests, host-side
# scripts and the test suite.
results/
docs/
deploy/
scripts/
tests/
CHANGELOG.md
CONTRIBUTING.md
.pre-commit-config.yaml

# Large regenerable artifacts a local run may leave in the checkout
*.onnx
*.engine
*.plan
*.trt
*.nsys-rep
*.ncu-rep
*.sqlite
# Anchored like .gitignore: only the repo-root adapters/ directory, never a source or
# fixture directory that happens to be named adapters/.
/adapters/
.hf_cache/
# `*.engine` above also matches the engine image's build file; BuildKit reads the
# Dockerfile out of band, but un-ignoring it keeps `docker build` honest either way.
!Dockerfile.engine

# Editors / OS
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store
